#doc(title: [Motion and Fields], subject: "A-level Physics, maths in notes")

= Uniform acceleration

A body that starts with speed $u$ and accelerates uniformly at $a$ for a time $t$ reaches the speed $v = u + a t$ and travels the distance $s = u t + 1/2 a t^2$, so that eliminating the time gives $v^2 = u^2 + 2 a s$ for any motion along a straight line with constant acceleration.

$ v &= u + a t <eq:v> \
  s &= u t + 1/2 a t^2 <eq:s> \
  v^2 &= u^2 + 2 a s <eq:v2> $

@eq:v2 follows from @eq:v and @eq:s. Newton's second law in its general form is

$ F = (dif p) / (dif t) = (m v - m u) / t $

and the work done by a varying force along a path from $x_1$ to $x_2$ is the integral $W = integral_(x_1)^(x_2) F(x) dif x$, which for a spring of stiffness $k$ gives $W = 1/2 k x^2$.

= Fields

The gravitational field strength at a distance $r$ from a mass $M$ is $g = (G M) / r^2$, and the potential is $V = -(G M) / r$. For a set of point masses the potential adds up:

$ V = -G sum_(i=1)^n m_i / abs(bold(r) - bold(r)_i) $

The magnitude of a vector $vec(E)$ with components $E_x$ and $E_y$ is $abs(vec(E)) = sqrt(E_x^2 + E_y^2)$, and its direction makes the angle $theta = arctan(E_y / E_x)$ with the $x$-axis. A rotation by $theta$ is the matrix

$ R(theta) = mat(cos theta, -sin theta; sin theta, cos theta) $

and the potential of a charged sphere of radius $R$ is

$ V(r) = cases((Q) / (4 pi epsilon_0 r) & r >= R, (Q) / (4 pi epsilon_0 R) & r < R) $

$:tex \lim_{n \to \infty} \left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right)^n = e $

= Standard form and signs

A wavelength of $5.0 times 10^-7$ m is $500$ nm, and a capacitor discharging through a resistor keeps the fraction $e^-1 approx 0.37$ of its charge after one time constant, since its charge falls as $Q = Q_0 e^(-t/(R C))$. In beta-minus decay a neutron becomes a proton, an electron $e^-$ and an antineutrino; in beta-plus decay a proton emits a positron $e^+$, and the ions $"Na"^+$ and $"Cl"^-$ form a salt. A slow neutron splits a nucleus of uranium-235 into barium, krypton and three neutrons:

$:tex {}^{235}_{92}\mathrm{U} + {}^{1}_{0}\mathrm{n} \to {}^{141}_{56}\mathrm{Ba} + {}^{92}_{36}\mathrm{Kr} + 3\,{}^{1}_{0}\mathrm{n} $

and a nucleus of uranium-238 decays by emitting an alpha particle: $""_92^238 "U" -> ""_90^234 "Th" + ""_2^4 alpha$.

= Data

#let c = 3.00e8

The constants of a data section are written as the author gives them: the speed of light in vacuum is #qty(3.00e8, "m s^-1"), the elementary charge #qty(1.60e-19, "C") and the Planck constant #qty(6.63e-34, "J s"), and a reading of #qty(2.0, "V") keeps its last figure. A value a script computes is written in the shorter of its plain and power-of-ten forms: light travels #qty(c * 2.0, "m") in two seconds and #qty(c / 200000.0, "m") in five microseconds. A resistor of #qty(4.7, "kohm") in series with one of #qty(2.2, "Mohm") has a total resistance of $R = qty(2.2047, "Mohm", digits: 3)$, and a contact resistance of #qty(15, "mohm") is negligible.
